From: Herrie <claude.ai@herrie.org>
Date: Fri, 19 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] halium: fail loudly when the rootfs mount fails

mountroot() mounted the rootfs and never looked at the result:

    mount -o loop,rw $imagefile /halium-system

If that failed, execution carried straight on with an empty /halium-system.
identify_android_image, the read-only remount and finally switch_root all
behave as though an empty directory were a real root, so the first visible
symptom is the system dying immediately after handover to /sbin/init - with
nothing in the log pointing at the mount.

Worse, the status mountroot reports was taken from the wrong command:

    tell_kmsg "mounting ... (image developer mode)"
    mountroot_status="$?"

$? there is the exit status of tell_kmsg, i.e. of echo, which is always 0. In
developer-image mode mountroot therefore reported success unconditionally,
whatever happened to the mount.

Check the mount, panic with the real rc, and require /sbin/init (or systemd)
to exist under the new root before calling it mounted - a bind of an empty
directory succeeds, and so does a loop mount of an image that is a valid
filesystem but not a rootfs. Take mountroot_status from something meaningful
in both branches, and warn if the read-only remount fails.

Upstream-Status: Pending

Signed-off-by: Herrie <claude.ai@herrie.org>
---
--- a/scripts/halium
+++ b/scripts/halium
@@ -500,14 +500,38 @@
 	mkdir -p /halium-system
 
 	tell_kmsg "mounting system rootfs at /halium-system"
+	_rootfs_rc=0
 	if [ -n "$_syspart" ]; then
 		mount -o rw $_syspart /halium-system
+		_rootfs_rc=$?
+		_rootfs_what="$_syspart"
 	elif [ -f "$imagefile" ]; then
 		# Rootfs is an image file
 		mount -o loop,rw $imagefile /halium-system
+		_rootfs_rc=$?
+		_rootfs_what="$imagefile (loop)"
 	elif [ -d "$imagefile" ]; then
 		# Rootfs is a directory
 		mount -o bind /tmpmnt/halium-rootfs /halium-system
+		_rootfs_rc=$?
+		_rootfs_what="/tmpmnt/halium-rootfs (bind)"
+	else
+		panic "No rootfs to mount: _syspart empty and $imagefile is neither a file nor a directory"
+	fi
+
+	# Fail loudly. This mount is the whole point of mountroot, and every later
+	# step - identify_android_image, the remount, switch_root - behaves as if an
+	# empty /halium-system were a real root, so a silent failure here surfaces
+	# much later as an unexplained death just after handover to /sbin/init.
+	if [ "$_rootfs_rc" != 0 ]; then
+		panic "Failed to mount $_rootfs_what at /halium-system (mount rc=$_rootfs_rc)"
+	fi
+
+	# A zero exit status is not proof the root is usable: a bind of an empty
+	# directory succeeds, and so does a loop mount of an image whose filesystem
+	# mounted but is not a rootfs. Require something every LuneOS rootfs has.
+	if [ ! -x /halium-system/sbin/init ] && [ ! -x /halium-system/usr/lib/systemd/systemd ] ; then
+		panic "Mounted $_rootfs_what at /halium-system but it has no /sbin/init - wrong image, or the filesystem is empty"
 	fi
 	
 	# Identify image mode: either "rootfs" or "system"
@@ -521,12 +545,17 @@
 	# /.writable_image exist, mount the rootfs as rw
 	if [ -e /tmpmnt/.writable_image ] || [ -e /halium-system/.writable_image ]; then
 		tell_kmsg "mounting $_syspart $imagefile (image developer mode)"
-		mountroot_status="$?"
+		# Nothing is run in this branch, so the root is as good as the mount
+		# above left it. It used to take $? from tell_kmsg - i.e. from echo -
+		# which is always 0, so mountroot reported success unconditionally.
+		mountroot_status=0
 	else
 		# Neither of those exist, remount read-only
 		tell_kmsg "mounting $_syspart $imagefile (user mode)"
 		mount -o remount,ro /halium-system
 		mountroot_status="$?"
+		[ "$mountroot_status" = 0 ] || \
+			tell_kmsg "WARNING: remount,ro of /halium-system failed (rc=$mountroot_status)"
 	fi
 
 	# Mount the android system partition to a temporary location
